::ALG1_U01_L06_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L06_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.1
Question:
Evaluate 3² times 3³.
{
~A. 27#This adds the exponents correctly but stops at 3³. Product of powers gives 3⁵, then evaluate.
=B. 243#Correct. 3² times 3³ = 3⁵ = 243.
~C. 729#This multiplies the exponents to get 3⁶. When multiplying powers with the same base, add the exponents.
~D. 18#This multiplies the visible numbers instead of using exponent properties.
}

::ALG1_U01_L06_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L06_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.2
Question:
Rewrite x⁴ times x⁵.
{
~A. x²⁰#This multiplies the exponents. Product of powers uses addition of exponents.
~B. 2x⁹#This adds an extra coefficient. The expression only combines powers of x.
~C. x#This subtracts exponents instead of adding them. Subtraction is used for quotients, not products.
=D. x⁹#Correct. The bases match, so add the exponents: 4 + 5 = 9.
}

::ALG1_U01_L06_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L06_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.2
Question:
Rewrite (a³)².
{
=A. a⁶#Correct. A power raised to a power uses multiplication of exponents: 3 times 2 = 6.
~B. a⁵#This adds the exponents. For a power raised to a power, multiply the exponents.
~C. a⁹#This squares the exponent 3. The outside exponent means multiply 3 by 2.
~D. 2a³#This treats the outside exponent like a coefficient. Exponents do not become coefficients.
}

::ALG1_U01_L06_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L06_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.1
Question:
Evaluate 64⁽¹⁄²⁾.
{
~A. 32#This divides 64 by 2. An exponent of 1/2 means square root, not divide by 2.
~B. 4#This is sqrt(16), not sqrt(64). Use the square root of 64.
=C. 8#Correct. 64⁽¹⁄²⁾ = sqrt(64) = 8.
~D. 128#This multiplies by 2. A rational exponent of 1/2 represents a root.
}

::ALG1_U01_L06_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID: ALG1_U01_L06_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FND.2
Question:
Rewrite z⁻³ with a positive exponent.
{
~A. z³#This changes the sign of the exponent without moving the factor. A negative exponent moves the factor to the denominator.
=B. 1/z³#Correct. z⁻³ = 1/z³.
~C. -z³#A negative exponent does not make the expression negative. It creates a reciprocal.
~D. z/3#This treats the exponent like division by 3. Use the reciprocal with z³ in the denominator.
}
